---
title: Overview
summary: Typed content collections for ogygia, from markdown files, JSON or a CMS, with markdown compiled through mdsvex and Shiki. On top of them sits a docs-site layer with a nav tree, search, sitemap and llms.txt, and ready-made docs and blog shells.
related:
  - content/collections
  - content/sites/site
  - regions/held-regions
---

`@ogygia/content` is where an ogygia app keeps what it publishes. A **collection** turns a folder of markdown, a JSON file or a CMS into typed entries, each with an `id`, its `data` (checked by your schema) and a `body`. The collection lives on the server, only plain data crosses the wire, and a body renders as a [region](/docs/regions/regions), so islands inside a markdown file hydrate like any other island.

On top of collections, `site()` arranges them into a site: a nav tree, prev/next, full-text search, a sitemap, `llms.txt` and a shell around each page. This docs site is built with it.

## Install

```bash
pnpm add @ogygia/content
```

Markdown files compile through Kit's config, the argument of `sveltekit()`, so `extensions()` and `preprocess()` go there, after `content()`:

```ts title=vite.config.ts
import { sveltekit } from '@sveltejs/kit/vite';
import { vitePreprocess } from '@sveltejs/vite-plugin-svelte';
import { ogygia } from 'ogygia/vite';
import { content, preprocess, extensions } from '@ogygia/content/vite';
import { defineConfig } from 'vite';

export default defineConfig({
  plugins: [
    ogygia(),
    content({ markdown: {} }),
    sveltekit({ extensions: extensions(), preprocess: [vitePreprocess(), ...preprocess()] })
  ]
});
```

The content macros bring their own types:

```ts title=src/ogygia.d.ts
/// <reference types="ogygia/types" />
/// <reference types="@ogygia/content/types" />
```

Nothing goes in `hooks.server.ts`. `npx ogygia init --markdown` writes all of this for you, and `npx ogygia site init` scaffolds a whole docs site ([CLI](/docs/cli/commands)).

## A first collection

Define the collection once, in a server module:

```ts title=src/lib/posts.server.ts
import { content } from '@ogygia/content';
import * as v from 'valibot';

export const posts = content({
  loader: import.meta.og.loader.markdown('../content/posts'),
  schema: v.object({ title: v.string(), date: v.string() })
});
```

A page reads an entry from a `.remote.ts`, and awaiting the body bakes it into a region ticket, the one form of a body that crosses the wire:

```ts title=src/lib/posts.remote.ts
import { query } from '$app/server';
import * as v from 'valibot';
import { posts } from './posts.server';

export const post = query(v.string(), async (id) => {
  const e = await posts.get(id);
  return e ? { data: e.data, body: e.body ? await e.body : undefined } : null;
});
```

## With ogygia's regions

The body is a held region, so the page renders it with `ogygia`'s `<Region>`. A component imported with a mark inside the markdown becomes an island on the page, and nothing else in the post ships JavaScript:

```svelte title=src/routes/blog/[slug]/+page.svelte
<script lang="ts">
  import { page } from '$app/state';
  import { Region } from 'ogygia';
  import { post } from '#lib/posts.remote';

  const view = (await post(page.params.slug))!;
</script>

<h1>{view.data.title}</h1>
<Region of={view.body} />
```

```svelte title=src/content/posts/hello.svx
---
title: Hello
date: 2026-01-01
---

<script>
  import Like from '#lib/Like.svelte' with { wake: 'visible' };
</script>

Everything here is static HTML, except this button: <Like />
```

[Held regions](/docs/regions/held-regions) explains what crosses and how, and [Timing & nesting](/docs/regions/timing-nesting) covers the `wake` schedules.

## With other packages

- **[@ogygia/postrender](/docs/postrender/overview).** A page built from a collection is usually the same for everyone, so postrender can store it on its first request. A CMS webhook calls `postrender.invalidate(path)` when an entry changes.
- **[@ogygia/router](/docs/router/overview).** When paths come from the collection rather than from files, a route table can map them, and a page's `entries` lists the slugs to prerender: `'/blog/[slug]': page(Post, { load, entries: async () => (await posts.refs()).map((p) => ({ slug: p.id })) })`.
- **[@ogygia/devtools](/docs/devtools/overview).** The profiler shows how long the markdown pass and the shell take in a server render.

